00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:07.390 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  one of the core findings of our research  is that the political arguments that are  going to be effective for building a  bigger consensus in the u.s. is usually  an argument you haven't heard before  it's an argument that advocates for a  policy or a candidate in terms of the  values and beliefs of the people that  are being targeted for persuasion  we've tested this idea both ways we've  looked at whether liberal policy  positions do better when they're framed  in terms of conservative values and we  find that they do because they pull  greater support from conservatives and  sometimes moderates and then we've also  looked at whether conservative policy  positions perform better when they're  reframed in terms of liberal values and  again we find that they do so for  example if you were to reframe military  spending as consistent with equality of  opportunity and social justice making  the case for the military as an  institution where the poor and  minorities can achieve on a relatively  level playing field with other Americans  liberals will be more open to supporting  high levels of military spending after  they hear an argument like that  a few years ago we got really interested  in same-sex marriage attitudes and we're  interested in whether certain persuasive  techniques might be more effective for  building support for same-sex marriage  and so to explore this idea we ran an  experiment where we had people either  read a relatively conventional argument  for same-sex marriage that said that gay  marriage should be legal because of the  values of equality fairness gay people  should have the same rights as anybody  else  we compared the effects of that message  to a very different message that made  the case for same-sex marriage in terms  of the values of patriotism and group  loyalty and it said that gay people are  proud patriotic Americans they serve in  the military they contribute to our  economy and our society and for that  reason they deserve the same rights as  anybody else and what we found is that  that second message was significantly  more persuasive and it was especially  more persuasive with conservatives who  are normally reluctant to support  same-sex marriage especially at the time  we ran the study a few years ago but  then when they heard that argument in  terms of their own values more  conservative values around patriotism  group loyalty they said oh okay you know  like this is starting to speak to me and  some of them moved their their positions  on these issues  most recently we ran an experiment on  how to make the most persuasive appeal  you could for an economically  progressive political candidate and  specifically a presidential candidate so  we're interested in is there a way to  build greater levels of support for  economically progressive candidates and  the reason we were interested in this is  that if you look historically Americans  are actually quite supportive of federal  jobs programs minimum wage increases  paid family leave you know these sorts  of programs they pull very well in  America but Americans don't necessarily  vote for the candidates that would pass  those policies and so they don't get  passed at a high rate so we're very  interested in this puzzle so what we did  was we constructed an experiment where  we had a candidate who supported these  pretty popular policies and we either  had them talk about those policies in  terms of classically liberal values like  equality social justice protecting  vulnerable people or we had them talk  about them in what are often thought to  be more conservative values of respect  for tradition caring for family the  American Dream things like that and what  we found was that candidate that second  candidate that talked in terms of more  conservative values but about  economically progressive policies was  clearly the most popular candidate in  the experiment that we ran they gained  extra support from moderates and  conservatives alike and they you know  we're essentially in the study we're an  you know a shoo-in to win in an election  one thing that I think is really  attractive about moral reframing is it  offers an alternative to our you know  our only real technique that we think of  now as a way to build political  consensus which is compromise compromise  on policy move to the perceived middle  and and that's gonna be the way to pick  up 51% of voters or get something passed  and that's an unsatisfying tool if it's  the only tool you have and so it's  attractive I think to have another  alternative another way that you could  add voters add people to your base of  support besides just changing the policy  positions you advocate for especially if  you think those are the right policy  positions  you think about Barack Obama Ronald  Reagan you know these are people who  could Intuit what a given audience  needed you know and say that idea that a  majority of people we're gonna turn out  to be moved by and potentially say  different things to different groups you  know we often think that if you're  saying different things different groups  that that means you're a flip-flopper or  you're insincere  or you're being cynical or something  like that but I think that that's not  really a realistic critique in a  polarized society in a polarized society  where people value different things they  have incredibly diverse backgrounds  we're gonna have to be comfortable with  the fact that people are gonna have  different reasons for agreeing on things  that people are going to come to major  issues like inequality or climate change  and potentially take the same side on  them but for very different reasons and  if we're not comfortable with that we're  not going to get political consensus  I think that if we're gonna resolve all  these tensions productively it's gonna  mean that we somehow turn towards one  another and saw some humanity across  political lines like maybe the existing  strategies worked so badly that we wound  up saying you know what we're not gonna  do it that way the politics of division  they're not working they're hurting the  country as a whole  and they're not really even helping my  side get a critical mass to pass  anything and so I'm gonna try a  different way and then that's gonna be  us turning towards each other and seeing  a common humanity that we struggle to  see now  [Music]

